  "hints": [
    "One row being incremented 20,000 times a second is the problem. How do you avoid hammering it?",
    "Reads of a hot count belong in memory; writes can be buffered and applied in batches.",
    "LB (4) → API (48) → Redis (3) → Cassandra (6); likes → Kafka → batch counters (14) → Cassandra."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 40,
    "costBudget": 7084
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Record each like as an event (user, post) so a user can like only once, then aggregate counts in batches instead of incrementing one row per like.",
    "Serve counts from a cache; for extreme hot keys, shard the counter into N sub-counters and sum them.",
    "The displayed count can lag by a second; the set of who liked what must be exact."
  ]
}
